.TH GP-SWITCH 1 "2026-09-21" "gitplus" "gitplus Manual"
.SH NAME
gp-switch \- everything git switch does, plus branch/worktree/PR resolution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gp switch
.RI ( BRANCH | WORKTREE | PR# | URL |.|@)
.RI [ "git switch flags" ]
.br
.B gp switch
.RI [ "git switch's own usage" ]
.br
.B gp switch
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gp switch
is a superset of
.BR "git switch" :
anything that changes what the positional argument(s) mean \(em
.BR \-c / \-C
(create a branch),
.BR \-\-detach / \-d ,
.BR \-\-orphan ,
an explicit
.B \-\-
end-of-options marker, git's own
.B \-
(previous branch) shorthand, or a start-point given alongside
.BR \-c / \-C
\(em is recognized by exact token and handed straight to the real
.B git switch
untouched. None of that grammar is reimplemented, so every flag
.B git switch
has works exactly as documented there, with nothing of gitplus's own to
drift out of sync with git's.
.PP
The plain case \(em one target, plus ordinary flags like
.B \-q
\(em is where this adds value. The target can be a branch name, a
.B WORKTREE
name (its directory name or full path), a PR number, a PR URL, or
.RI ' . '/' @ '
(the current branch). Naming the worktree is often what you actually
remember \(em the branch inside it is the thing you were trying to look up.
A real branch name always wins if a branch and a worktree share a name.
.PP
A branch can only ever be checked out in one worktree at a time (a hard git
rule) \(em normally a hard stop:
.B "gp pr"
and
.B "gp sync"
just refuse and report where the branch already is. If the resolved target
is checked out in
.I another
worktree,
.B gp switch
instead frees it: it detaches that
.I other
worktree's HEAD at its current commit (safe \(em doesn't discard anything,
it just stops it holding the branch name), then checks the branch out in the
.B current
worktree. This is one\-way: your current branch is
.B not
relocated to that other worktree, which is left on a detached HEAD at the
commit it already had.
.PP
Uncommitted changes (tracked or untracked) in that other worktree come with
you: they are stashed there and popped back here right after the checkout
succeeds, so switching to a branch to work on it does not leave the work in
progress behind on a different worktree. A pop that conflicts is left for
you to resolve by hand \(em git keeps the stash entry rather than dropping
it, so nothing is lost either way.
.PP
.B "gp pr <id>"
uses the same free-it-first logic internally, so checking out a PR whose
branch is checked out elsewhere just works instead of refusing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.PP
Every other flag
.B git switch
accepts is passed through \(em see
.BR git-switch (1)
for the full list.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gp switch spec-first
Free
.I spec-first
from wherever it's checked out and switch to it here.
.TP
.B gp switch 132
Same, but naming a PR number instead of its head branch.
.TP
.B gp switch wt-hotfix
Same, but naming the worktree that
.I spec-first
happens to be checked out in.
.TP
.B gp switch -c new-feature
Passed straight through to
.BR "git switch -c new-feature" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-switch (1),
.BR gp-sync (1),
.BR gp-pr (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
